Component Breakdown\par
Client Layer (Frontend):\par
\par
Built with React and Ant Design for a clean, professional engineering control panel.\par
\par
Features an integrated Three.js / WebGL 3D Interactive Canvas that dynamically constructs and animates the robot's 3D meshes in real-time.\par
\par
Orchestration Layer (Backend):\par
\par
Powered by a robust FastAPI / Django async engine that manages multi-step agent execution loops, data caching, and strict JSON schema validation.\par
\par
AI Intelligence Layer (Meta Model API):\par
\par
Muse Spark / Llama: Acts as the cognitive engine for architectural reasoning, mission evaluation, and structured Bill of Materials (BOM) compilation.\par
\par
Muse Code: Specialized for writing flawless, idiomatic URDF configuration files, kinematic solver equations, and target microcontroller firmware (C++ / Python / ROS2 nodes).\par
\par
SAM 3.1: Processes optional uploaded technical sketches, hand-drawn blueprints, or hardware schematics, segmenting components to extract layout constraints.\par
\par
Simulation Layer:\par
\par
A browser-based physics runner that reads the generated URDF structure, applies collision boundaries, and tests physical performance (center of gravity, torque limits, drive dynamics) directly in the browser.\par
\par
3. How the Project is Meant to Run (End-to-End Workflow)\par
The user journey through the application follows a seamless, step-by-step automated pipeline:\par
\par
Step 1: Natural Language Intent Ingestion\par
The user opens the dashboard and inputs a mission brief into the prompt bar, for example:\par
\par
"Design a 4-wheeled autonomous surveillance rover with a 2-DOF pan-tilt camera gimbal."\par
\par
Alternatively, the user can upload a reference sketch, which SAM 3.1 instantly parses and segments to extract structural constraints.\par
\par
Step 2: Cognitive Spec & BOM Compilation\par
The backend invokes Muse Spark under strict JSON schema rules.\par
\par
The model analyzes the physical requirements, computes actuator specifications, and generates a structured Bill of Materials (BOM) (listing microcontrollers, motor types, chassis dimensions, and power sources) alongside a kinematic tree topology.\par
\par
Step 3: Automated Code & URDF Generation\par
Once the BOM is locked, the orchestration engine triggers Muse Code.\par
\par
The system streams the generation of two critical assets:\par
\par
The URDF XML File: Defining links, joints, inertias, and visual primitives.\par
\par
The Control Firmware: Generating Python/C++ code snippets for motor drivers and sensor feedback loops.\par
\par
Step 4: Digital Twin Assembly & Physics Simulation\par
The frontend receives the compiled URDF text payload.\par
\par
The Three.js Canvas dynamically parses the XML tree, instantiates 3D geometric meshes, and links them via simulated joints.\par
\par
The user hits "Run Simulation", activating the physics sandbox. They can watch the rover drive, test turning radiuses, and observe the camera gimbal pivot in response to simulated commands.\par
\par
Step 5: Export & Handover\par
Satisfied with the virtual prototype, the user clicks "Export Package", downloading a complete ZIP containing the structured BOM, URDF file, ROS2/Microcontroller code, and system architecture report ready for real-world manufacturing or further hardware refinement.\par
\par
Ready to Code?\par
This architecture hits every checkmark for the "Push the Frontier" track: it introduces multi-step agent reasoning, multimodal vision processing (SAM 3.1), advanced code generation (Muse Code), and interactive 3D WebGL rendering.\par
